<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Concluding discussion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have developed methodology that gives a fully automatic
nonparametric density estimate under the condition that the density is
log-concave, and shown how it may be extended to fit finite mixtures
of log-concave densities. We have indicated a wide range of possible
applications, including classification, clustering and functional
estimation problems. The area of shape-constrained estimation is
currently undergoing rapid growth, as evidenced by the many recent
publications cited in the penultimate paragraph of
Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, as well as recent workshops in Oberwolfach
(November 2006), Eindhoven (October 2007) and Bristol (November 2007).
We hope that this paper will stimulate further interest and research
in the field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As well as the continued development and refinement of the
computational algorithms and graphical displays of estimates, and
studies of theoretical performance, there remain many challenges and
interesting directions for future research. These include:</p>
<ol id="S8.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Studying other shape constraints. These have received some
attention for univariate data, dating back to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grenander (1956)</a></cite>,
but much less in the multivariate setting.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Developing both formal and informal diagnostic tools for
assessing the validity of shape constraints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assessing the uncertainty in shape-constrained nonparametric
density estimates, through confidence intervals/bands.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Developing analogous methodology for discrete data from
shape-constrained distributions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(v)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Examining nonparametric shape constraints in regression
problems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(vi)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Studying methods for choosing the number of clusters in
nonparametric, shape-constrained mixture models.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Glossary of terms and results from convex analysis and
computational geometry</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All of the definitions and results below can be found in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee (1997)</a></cite>. The <em id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">epigraph</em> of a
function <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{k}\rightarrow[-\infty,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{k}\rightarrow[-\infty,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> is the set</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{epi}(f)=\{(x,\mu):x\in\mathbb{R}^{k},\mu\in\mathbb{R},\mu\leq f(x)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>epi</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{epi}(f)=\{(x,\mu):x\in\mathbb{R}^{k},\mu\in\mathbb{R},\mu\leq f(x)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We say <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concave</em> if its epigraph is non-empty and
convex as a subset of <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>; note that this agrees with
the terminology of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barndorff-Nielsen (1978)</a></cite>, but is
what <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite> calls a <em id="A1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper concave</em> function.
If <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex subset of <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> then provided <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f:C\rightarrow[-\infty,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow[-\infty,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> is not identically <math id="A1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, it is
<em id="A1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concave</em> if and only if</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\bigl(tx+(1-t)y\bigr)\geq tf(x)+(1-t)f(y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\bigl(tx+(1-t)y\bigr)\geq tf(x)+(1-t)f(y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="A1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. A non-negative function <math id="A1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is
<em id="A1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">log-concave</em> if <math id="A1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f</annotation></semantics></math> is concave, with the convention that
<math id="A1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log 0=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log 0=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="A1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">support</em> of a log-concave function <math id="A1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>
is <math id="A1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{k}:\log f(x)&gt;-\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{k}:\log f(x)&gt;-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a convex subset of
<math id="A1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A subset <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">affine</em> if <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tx+(1-t)y\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tx+(1-t)y\in M</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">affine hull</em> of
<math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{aff}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>aff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{aff}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the smallest affine set containing
<math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Every non-empty affine set <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<em id="A1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel</em> to a unique subspace of <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that
there is a unique subspace <math id="A1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=L+a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=L+a</annotation></semantics></math>,
for some <math id="A1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="A1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dimension</em> of <math id="A1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the
dimension of this subspace, and more generally the dimension of a
non-empty convex set is the dimension of its affine hull. A finite
set of points <math id="A1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\{x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\{x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">affinely
independent</em> if <math id="A1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{aff}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>aff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{aff}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional. The
<em id="A1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative interior</em> of a convex set <math id="A1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the interior which
results when we regard <math id="A1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> as a subset of its affine hull. The
<em id="A1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative boundary</em> of <math id="A1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the set difference between its
closure and its relative interior. If <math id="A1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine set in
<math id="A1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, then an <em id="A1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">affine transformation</em> (or <em id="A1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">afffine
function</em>) is a function <math id="A1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:M\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:M\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\bigl(tx+(1-t)y\bigr)=tT(x)+(1-t)T(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\bigl(tx+(1-t)y\bigr)=tT(x)+(1-t)T(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in M</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A1.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">closure</em> of a concave function <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
denoted <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cl}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cl}(g)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the function whose epigraph is the
closure in <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{epi}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>epi</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{epi}(g)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is the least
upper semi-continuous, concave function satisfying <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cl}(g)\geq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cl}(g)\geq g</annotation></semantics></math>. The function <math id="A1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">closed</em> if <math id="A1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cl}(g)=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cl}(g)=g</annotation></semantics></math>.
An arbitrary function <math id="A1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">continuous
relative</em> to a subset <math id="A1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> if its restriction to <math id="A1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>
is a continuous function. A non-zero vector <math id="A1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<em id="A1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">direction of increase</em> of <math id="A1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\mapsto h(x+tz)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\mapsto h(x+tz)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-decreasing for every <math id="A1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The convex hull of finitely many points is called a <em id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polytope</em>.
The convex hull of <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1</annotation></semantics></math> affinely independent points is called a
<math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><em id="A1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">-dimensional simplex</em> (pl. <em id="A1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">simplices</em>). If <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a
convex set in <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, then a <em id="A1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">supporting half-space</em> to
<math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed half-space which contains <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and has a point of <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
in its boundary. A <em id="A1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">supporting hyperplane</em> <math id="A1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a
hyperplane which is the boundary of a supporting half-space to <math id="A1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="A1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}:\langle x,b\rangle=\beta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}:\langle x,b\rangle=\beta\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for
some <math id="A1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x,b\rangle\leq\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x,b\rangle\leq\beta</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in C</annotation></semantics></math> with equality for
at least one <math id="A1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of points in <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\mathrm{conv}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\mathrm{conv}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polytope in <math id="A1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then a <em id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">face</em> of <math id="A1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of the form <math id="A1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cap H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cap H</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>
is a supporting hyperplane to <math id="A1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="A1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vertex set</em> of <math id="A1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
denoted <math id="A1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vert}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vert</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vert}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the set of <math id="A1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional faces
(<em id="A1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vertices</em>) of <math id="A1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="A1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">subdivision</em> of <math id="A1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set
of <math id="A1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polytopes <math id="A1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{S_{1},\ldots,S_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{1},\ldots,S_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the
union of <math id="A1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\ldots,S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\ldots,S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the intersection of any two distinct
polytopes in the subdivision is a face of both of them. If <math id="A1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{S_{1},\ldots,S_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{S_{1},\ldots,S_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}=\{\tilde{S}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{S}_{t^{\prime}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}=\{\tilde{S}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{S}_{t^{\prime}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are two subdivisions of <math id="A1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="A1.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="A1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">refinement</em> of <math id="A1.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> if each <math id="A1.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is
contained in some <math id="A1.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}_{l^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}_{l^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="A1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trivial subdivision</em> of
<math id="A1.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="A1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">triangulation</em> of <math id="A1.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdivision of <math id="A1.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
in which each polytope is a simplex.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polytope in <math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a
<math id="A1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional face of <math id="A1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, then there
is a unique supporting hyperplane <math id="A1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="A1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. The
polytope <math id="A1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in exactly one of the closed half-spaces
determined by <math id="A1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and if <math id="A1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is in the opposite open half-space, then
<math id="A1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">visible</em> from <math id="A1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set in
<math id="A1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\mathrm{conv}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\mathrm{conv}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="A1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{S_{1},\ldots,S_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{S_{1},\ldots,S_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subdivision of <math id="A1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, then the result of
<em id="A1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pushing</em> <math id="A1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is the subdivision <math id="A1.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by
modifying each <math id="A1.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{l}\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{l}\in S</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<ol id="A1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\notin S_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\notin S_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{l}\in\tilde{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{l}\in\tilde{S}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in S_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in S_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{conv}(\mathrm{vert}(S_{l})\setminus\{v\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vert</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{conv}(\mathrm{vert}(S_{l})\setminus\{v\})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional, then <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{l}\in\tilde{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{l}\in\tilde{S}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in S_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in S_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{l}^{\prime}=\mathrm{conv}(\mathrm{vert}(S_{l})\setminus\{v\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vert</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{l}^{\prime}=\mathrm{conv}(\mathrm{vert}(S_{l})\setminus\{v\})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional, then <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{l}^{\prime}\in\tilde{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{l}^{\prime}\in\tilde{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also, if <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is any <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional face of <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{l}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{l}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that is visible
from <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{conv}(F\cup\{v\})\in\tilde{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{conv}(F\cup\{v\})\in\tilde{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex function on <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="A1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">subgradient</em> of <math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> if</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(z)\geq\sigma(y)+\langle y^{\prime},z-y\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(z)\geq\sigma(y)+\langle y^{\prime},z-y\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="A1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable at
<math id="A1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\sigma(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\sigma(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique subgradient to <math id="A1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> at
<math id="A1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise the set of subgradients at <math id="A1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> has more than one
element. The <em id="A1.p7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">one-sided directional derivative</em> of <math id="A1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> at
<math id="A1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="A1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(y;z)=\lim_{t\searrow 0}\frac{\sigma(y+tz)-\sigma(y)}{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>↘</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(y;z)=\lim_{t\searrow 0}\frac{\sigma(y+tz)-\sigma(y)}{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p7.3" class="ltx_p">which always exists (allowing <math id="A1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as limits)
provided <math id="A1.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is finite.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proofs</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Log-concave densities: examples, applications and properties ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
<br class="ltx_break"></span>(a) If <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave, then for <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can write</p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{X|P_{V}(X)}(x|t)\propto f(x)\mathbbm{1}_{\{P_{V}(x)=t\}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X|P_{V}(X)}(x|t)\propto f(x)\mathbbm{1}_{\{P_{V}(x)=t\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">a product of log-concave functions. Thus <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{X|P_{V}(X)}(\cdot|t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X|P_{V}(X)}(\cdot|t)</annotation></semantics></math>
is log-concave for each <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(b) Let <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct and let <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace of <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
whose orthogonal complement is <em id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel</em> to the <em id="A2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">affine
hull</em> of <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{1},x_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{1},x_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. the line through <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Writing <math id="A2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{P_{V}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{P_{V}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math> for the marginal density of <math id="A2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{V}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{V}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for
the common value of <math id="A2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{V}(x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{V}(x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{V}(x_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{V}(x_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the density of <math id="A2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at
<math id="A2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=f_{X|P_{V}(X)}(x|t)f_{P_{V}(X)}(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=f_{X|P_{V}(X)}(x|t)f_{P_{V}(X)}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="A2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave, as required.  <math id="A2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Completion of the Proof of
Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3 Existence, uniqueness and structure of the maximum likelihood estimator ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
<br class="ltx_break"></span>We prove each of the steps (i)–(v)
outlined in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Existence, uniqueness and structure of the maximum likelihood estimator ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in turn. First note that if
<math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then by Carathéodory’s theorem (Theorem 17.1 of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite>), there exist distinct indices
<math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},\ldots,i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},\ldots,i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq d+1</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=\sum_{l=1}^{r}\lambda_{l}X_{i_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=\sum_{l=1}^{r}\lambda_{l}X_{i_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math> with each <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{l}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{l}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{l=1}^{r}\lambda_{l}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{l=1}^{r}\lambda_{l}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, if <math id="A2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x_{0})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x_{0})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then by Jensen’s inequality,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty=\log f(x_{0})\geq\sum_{l=1}^{r}\lambda_{l}\log f(X_{i_{l}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty=\log f(x_{0})\geq\sum_{l=1}^{r}\lambda_{l}\log f(X_{i_{l}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">so <math id="A2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X_{i})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X_{i})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. But then <math id="A2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}(f)=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}(f)=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
This proves (i).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now suppose <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x_{0})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x_{0})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\notin C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\notin C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x:f(x)&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x:f(x)&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex set containing <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}\cup\{x_{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}\cup\{x_{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a set which has
strictly larger <math id="A2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Lebesgue measure than that of <math id="A2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We therefore have <math id="A2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}(f)&lt;\psi_{n}(f\mathbbm{1}_{C_{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}(f)&lt;\psi_{n}(f\mathbbm{1}_{C_{n}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which
proves (ii).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To prove (iii), we first show that <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">closed</em>. Suppose
that <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f(X_{i})=y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f(X_{i})=y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> but that <math id="A2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f\neq\bar{h}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f\neq\bar{h}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then since <math id="A2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f(x)\geq\bar{h}_{y}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f(x)\geq\bar{h}_{y}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume that there exists <math id="A2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f(x_{0})&gt;\bar{h}_{y}(x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f(x_{0})&gt;\bar{h}_{y}(x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is in the <em id="A2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative
interior</em> of <math id="A2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then since <math id="A2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> are continuous
at <math id="A2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (by Theorem 10.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite>), we must have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}(f)&lt;\psi_{n}\bigl(\exp(\bar{h}_{y})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}(f)&lt;\psi_{n}\bigl(\exp(\bar{h}_{y})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The only remaining possibility is that <math id="A2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is on the
<em id="A2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative boundary</em> of <math id="A2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="A2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed by
Corollary 17.2.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite>, so writing
<math id="A2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cl}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cl}(g)</annotation></semantics></math> for the <em id="A2.p5.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">closure</em> of a concave function <math id="A2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, we
have <math id="A2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{y}=\mathrm{cl}(\bar{h}_{y})=\mathrm{cl}(\log f)\geq\log f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cl</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y}=\mathrm{cl}(\bar{h}_{y})=\mathrm{cl}(\log f)\geq\log f</annotation></semantics></math>, where we have used Corollary 7.3.4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite>
to obtain the middle equality. It follows that <math id="A2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f</annotation></semantics></math> is closed and
<math id="A2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f=\bar{h}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f=\bar{h}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, which proves (iii).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f</annotation></semantics></math> has no <em id="A2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">direction of increase</em>, because if <math id="A2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-zero vector and <math id="A2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough that
<math id="A2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+tz\notin C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+tz\notin C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty=\log f(x+tz)&lt;\log f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty=\log f(x+tz)&lt;\log f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. It
follows by Theorem 27.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite> that the supremum
of <math id="A2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is finite (and is attained). Using properties (i) and (ii) as
well, we may write <math id="A2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int f(x)\,dx=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int f(x)\,dx=c</annotation></semantics></math>, say, where <math id="A2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=c\bar{f}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=c\bar{f}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="A2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}\in\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}\in\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. But then</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}(\bar{f})-\psi_{n}(f)=-1-\log c+c\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}(\bar{f})-\psi_{n}(f)=-1-\log c+c\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">with equality only if <math id="A2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves (iv).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To prove (v), we may assume by (iv) that <math id="A2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(\bar{h}_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(\bar{h}_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> is a
density. Let <math id="A2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{i}\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{i}\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})=M</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{i}\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{i}\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})=m</annotation></semantics></math>. We show that when <math id="A2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is large, in order for
<math id="A2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(\bar{h}_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(\bar{h}_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> to be a density, <math id="A2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> must be negative with <math id="A2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|m|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|m|</annotation></semantics></math> so
large that <math id="A2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}\bigl(\exp(\bar{h}_{y})\bigr)\leq\psi_{n}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}\bigl(\exp(\bar{h}_{y})\bigr)\leq\psi_{n}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>. First
observe that if <math id="A2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})=M</annotation></semantics></math>, then for <math id="A2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
sufficiently large we must have <math id="A2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M-m&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M-m&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and then</p>
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{h}_{y}\Bigl(X_{i}+\frac{1}{M-m}(x-X_{i})\Bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{h}_{y}\Bigl(X_{i}+\frac{1}{M-m}(x-X_{i})\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\frac{1}{M-m}\bar{h}_{y}(x)+\frac{M-m-1}{M-m}\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\frac{1}{M-m}\bar{h}_{y}(x)+\frac{M-m-1}{M-m}\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\frac{m}{M-m}+\frac{(M-m-1)M}{M-m}=M-1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\frac{m}{M-m}+\frac{(M-m-1)M}{M-m}=M-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">(The fact that <math id="A2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{y}(x)\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y}(x)\geq m</annotation></semantics></math> follows by Jensen’s
inequality.) Hence, denoting Lebesgue measure on <math id="A2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="A2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\{x:\bar{h}_{y}(x)\geq M-1\})\geq\mu\Bigl(\Bigl\{X_{i}+\frac{1}{M-m}(C_{n}-X_{i})\Bigr\}\Bigr)=\frac{\mu(C_{n})}{(M-m)^{d}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\{x:\bar{h}_{y}(x)\geq M-1\})\geq\mu\Bigl(\Bigl\{X_{i}+\frac{1}{M-m}(C_{n}-X_{i})\Bigr\}\Bigr)=\frac{\mu(C_{n})}{(M-m)^{d}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p7.3" class="ltx_p">Thus</p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\exp\{\bar{h}_{y}(x)\}\,dx\geq e^{M-1}\frac{\mu(C_{n})}{(M-m)^{d}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\exp\{\bar{h}_{y}(x)\}\,dx\geq e^{M-1}\frac{\mu(C_{n})}{(M-m)^{d}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p7.4" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(\bar{h}_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(\bar{h}_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> to be a density, then, we require <math id="A2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq-\frac{1}{2}e^{(M-1)/d}\mu(C_{n})^{1/d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq-\frac{1}{2}e^{(M-1)/d}\mu(C_{n})^{1/d}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is large. But then</p>
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}\bigl(\exp(\bar{h}_{y})\bigr)\leq\frac{(n-1)M}{n}-\frac{1}{2n}e^{(M-1)/d}\mu(C_{n})^{1/d}\leq\psi_{n}(f)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}\bigl(\exp(\bar{h}_{y})\bigr)\leq\frac{(n-1)M}{n}-\frac{1}{2n}e^{(M-1)/d}\mu(C_{n})^{1/d}\leq\psi_{n}(f)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p7.5" class="ltx_p">when <math id="A2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large. This proves (v).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">It is not hard to see that for any <math id="A2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the function <math id="A2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\mapsto\psi_{n}(\exp(\bar{h}_{y})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\mapsto\psi_{n}(\exp(\bar{h}_{y})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous on the compact set
<math id="A2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-M,M]^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-M,M]^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the proof of the existence of a maximum
likelihood estimator is complete. To prove uniqueness, suppose that
<math id="A2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},f_{2}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},f_{2}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and both <math id="A2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> maximise
<math id="A2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>. We may assume <math id="A2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},f_{2}\in\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},f_{2}\in\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log f_{1},\log f_{2}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log f_{1},\log f_{2}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are supported on <math id="A2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the normalised geometric mean</p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=\frac{\{f_{1}(x)f_{2}(x)\}^{1/2}}{\int_{C_{n}}\{f_{1}(y)f_{2}(y)\}^{1/2}\,dy}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=\frac{\{f_{1}(x)f_{2}(x)\}^{1/2}}{\int_{C_{n}}\{f_{1}(y)f_{2}(y)\}^{1/2}\,dy},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">is a log-concave density, with</p>
<table id="A2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi_{n}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi_{n}(g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f_{1}(X_{i})+\frac{1}{2n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f_{2}(X_{i})-\log\int_{C_{n}}\{f_{1}(y)f_{2}(y)\}^{1/2}\,dy-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f_{1}(X_{i})+\frac{1}{2n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log f_{2}(X_{i})-\log\int_{C_{n}}\{f_{1}(y)f_{2}(y)\}^{1/2}\,dy-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\psi_{n}(f_{1})-\log\int_{C_{n}}\{f_{1}(y)f_{2}(y)\}^{1/2}\,dy." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\psi_{n}(f_{1})-\log\int_{C_{n}}\{f_{1}(y)f_{2}(y)\}^{1/2}\,dy.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p8.3" class="ltx_p">However, by Cauchy–Schwarz, <math id="A2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{C_{n}}\{f_{1}(y)f_{2}(y)\}^{1/2}\,dy\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{C_{n}}\{f_{1}(y)f_{2}(y)\}^{1/2}\,dy\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}(g)\geq\psi_{n}(f_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}(g)\geq\psi_{n}(f_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equality is obtained if and only if <math id="A2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> almost everywhere, but
since <math id="A2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="A2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">continuous relative</em> to <math id="A2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Theorem 10.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite>), this implies that
<math id="A2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. An alternative way of proving the uniqueness of the
maximum likelihood estimator may be based on the fact that
<math id="A2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}\bigl(tf_{1}+(1-t)f_{2}\bigr)&gt;t\psi_{n}(f_{1})+(1-t)\psi_{n}(f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}\bigl(tf_{1}+(1-t)f_{2}\bigr)&gt;t\psi_{n}(f_{1})+(1-t)\psi_{n}(f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="A2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, provided <math id="A2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct elements
of <math id="A2.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.  <math id="A2.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 4.1 Reformulation ‣ 4 Computation of the maximum likelihood estimator ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>
<br class="ltx_break"></span>For <math id="A2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(1)},y^{(2)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(1)},y^{(2)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the function
<math id="A2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{ty^{(1)}+(1-t)y^{(2)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{ty^{(1)}+(1-t)y^{(2)}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the least concave function
satisfying <math id="A2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{ty^{(1)}+(1-t)y^{(2)}}(X_{i})\geq ty_{i}^{(1)}+(1-t)y_{i}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{ty^{(1)}+(1-t)y^{(2)}}(X_{i})\geq ty_{i}^{(1)}+(1-t)y_{i}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="A2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{ty^{(1)}+(1-t)y^{(2)}}\leq t\bar{h}_{y^{(1)}}+(1-t)\bar{h}_{y^{(2)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{ty^{(1)}+(1-t)y^{(2)}}\leq t\bar{h}_{y^{(1)}}+(1-t)\bar{h}_{y^{(2)}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The convexity of <math id="A2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> follows from this
and the convexity of the exponential function. It is clear that
<math id="A2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\geq\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\geq\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="A2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})\geq y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y}(X_{i})\geq y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="A2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">From Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3 Existence, uniqueness and structure of the maximum likelihood estimator ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we can find <math id="A2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="A2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\hat{f}_{n}=\bar{h}_{y^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\hat{f}_{n}=\bar{h}_{y^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{y^{*}}(X_{i})=y_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y^{*}}(X_{i})=y_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>, and this <math id="A2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> minimises <math id="A2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. For any
other <math id="A2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> which minimises <math id="A2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, by the uniqueness
part of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 3 Existence, uniqueness and structure of the maximum likelihood estimator ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we must have <math id="A2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}_{y}=\bar{h}_{y^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y}=\bar{h}_{y^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="A2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(y)&gt;\sigma(y^{*})=\tau(y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(y)&gt;\sigma(y^{*})=\tau(y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.  <math id="A2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Non-differentiability of <math id="A2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
and computation of subgradients</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we find explicitly the set of points at which the
function <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1 Reformulation ‣ 4 Computation of the maximum likelihood estimator ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) is differentiable,
and compute a subgradient of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> at each point. For
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J_{i}=\{j=(j_{1},\ldots,j_{d+1})\in J:i=j_{l}\ \text{for some
$l=1,\ldots,d+1$}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mtext>for some </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}=\{j=(j_{1},\ldots,j_{d+1})\in J:i=j_{l}\ \text{for some
$l=1,\ldots,d+1$}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the index set of those simplices <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n,j}</annotation></semantics></math> that
have <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as a vertex. Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of vectors <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{n})\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that for each <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=(j_{1},\ldots,j_{d+1})\in J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=(j_{1},\ldots,j_{d+1})\in J</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> then</p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\{(X_{i},y_{i}),(X_{j_{1}},y_{j_{1}}),\ldots,(X_{j_{d+1}},y_{j_{d+1}})\bigr\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\{(X_{i},y_{i}),(X_{j_{1}},y_{j_{1}}),\ldots,(X_{j_{d+1}},y_{j_{d+1}})\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is affinely independent in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the set of
points for which no tent pole is touching but not critically
supporting the tent. Notice that the complement of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> has
zero Lebesgue measure in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>, and in the notation of Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Computation of the maximum likelihood estimator ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>,
let</p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{i}(y)=-\frac{1}{n}+\sum_{j\in J_{i}}|\det A_{j}|\int_{T_{d}}e^{\langle w,z_{j}\rangle+y_{j_{1}}}\biggl\{\Bigl(1-\sum_{l=1}^{d}w_{l}\Bigr)\mathbbm{1}_{\{j_{1}=i\}}+\sum_{l=1}^{d}w_{l}\mathbbm{1}_{\{j_{l+1}=i\}}\biggr\}\,dw." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{i}(y)=-\frac{1}{n}+\sum_{j\in J_{i}}|\det A_{j}|\int_{T_{d}}e^{\langle w,z_{j}\rangle+y_{j_{1}}}\biggl\{\Bigl(1-\sum_{l=1}^{d}w_{l}\Bigr)\mathbbm{1}_{\{j_{1}=i\}}+\sum_{l=1}^{d}w_{l}\mathbbm{1}_{\{j_{l+1}=i\}}\biggr\}\,dw.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <span id="Thmthm4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A1)</span>. (a) For <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the function <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable at <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and for
<math id="Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\sigma}{\partial y_{i}}(y)=\partial_{i}(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\sigma}{\partial y_{i}}(y)=\partial_{i}(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b) For <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, the function <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is not
differentiable at <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, but the vector
<math id="Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\partial_{1}(y),\ldots,\partial_{n}(y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\partial_{1}(y),\ldots,\partial_{n}(y))</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgradient of <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> at
<math id="Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem 25.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite>, it suffices
to show that for <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, all of the partial derivatives
exist and are given by the expression in the statement of the
proposition. For <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(t)}=y+te_{i}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(t)}=y+te_{i}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th unit coordinate vector in
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For sufficiently small values of <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|t|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|t|</annotation></semantics></math>, we may write</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bar{h}_{y^{(t)}}(x)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\langle x,b_{j}^{(t)}\rangle-\beta_{j}^{(t)}&amp;\mbox{\text{if $x\in C_{n,j}$
for some $j\in J$}}\\
-\infty&amp;\mbox{if $x\notin C_{n}$,}\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mtext> for some </mtext><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}_{y^{(t)}}(x)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\langle x,b_{j}^{(t)}\rangle-\beta_{j}^{(t)}&amp;\mbox{\text{if $x\in C_{n,j}$
for some $j\in J$}}\\
-\infty&amp;\mbox{if $x\notin C_{n}$,}\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">for certain values of <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}^{(t)},\ldots,b_{m}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}^{(t)},\ldots,b_{m}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}^{(t)},\ldots,\beta_{m}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}^{(t)},\ldots,\beta_{m}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\notin J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\notin J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}^{(t)}=b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}^{(t)}=b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{j}^{(t)}=\beta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{j}^{(t)}=\beta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for
sufficiently small <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|t|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|t|</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, if <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then there
are two cases to consider:</span></p>
<ol id="A2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}=i</annotation></semantics></math>, then for sufficiently small <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}^{(t)}=z_{j}-t1_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}^{(t)}=z_{j}-t1_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-vector of ones,
so that <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(t)}_{j}=b_{j}-t(A_{j}^{T})^{-1}1_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(t)}_{j}=b_{j}-t(A_{j}^{T})^{-1}1_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{(t)}_{j}=\beta_{j}-t(1+\langle A_{j}^{-1}\alpha_{j},1_{d}\rangle)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{(t)}_{j}=\beta_{j}-t(1+\langle A_{j}^{-1}\alpha_{j},1_{d}\rangle)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{l+1}=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{l+1}=i</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\{1,\ldots,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\{1,\ldots,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then for
sufficiently small <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}^{(t)}=z_{j}+te_{l}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}^{(t)}=z_{j}+te_{l}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
<math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(t)}_{j}=b_{j}+t(A_{j}^{T})^{-1}e_{l}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(t)}_{j}=b_{j}+t(A_{j}^{T})^{-1}e_{l}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{j}^{(t)}=\beta_{j}+t\langle A_{j}^{-1}\alpha_{j},e_{l}^{d}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{j}^{(t)}=\beta_{j}+t\langle A_{j}^{-1}\alpha_{j},e_{l}^{d}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\frac{\partial\sigma}{\partial y_{i}}(y)&amp;=-\frac{1}{n}+\lim_{t\rightarrow 0}\frac{1}{t}\sum_{j\in J_{i}}\int_{C_{n,j}}\exp\bigl\{\langle x,b_{j}^{(t)}\rangle-\beta_{j}^{(t)}\bigr\}-\exp\left\{\langle x,b_{j}\rangle-\beta_{j}\right\}\,dx\\
&amp;=-\frac{1}{n}+\lim_{t\rightarrow 0}\frac{1}{t}\sum_{j\in J_{i}}\left[\int_{C_{n,j}}e^{\langle x,b_{j}\rangle-\beta_{j}}\bigl\{e^{t(1-\langle A_{j}^{-1}(x-\alpha_{j}),1_{d}\rangle)}-1\bigr\}\,dx\mathbbm{1}_{\{j_{1}=i\}}\right.\\
&amp;\hskip 142.26378pt+\left.\sum_{l=1}^{d}\int_{C_{n,j}}e^{\langle x,b_{j}\rangle-\beta_{j}}\bigl\{e^{t\langle A_{j}^{-1}(x-\alpha_{j}),e_{l}^{d}\rangle}-1\bigr\}\,dx\mathbbm{1}_{\{j_{l+1}=i\}}\right]\\
&amp;=\partial_{i}(y),\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">}</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">}</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\frac{\partial\sigma}{\partial y_{i}}(y)&amp;=-\frac{1}{n}+\lim_{t\rightarrow 0}\frac{1}{t}\sum_{j\in J_{i}}\int_{C_{n,j}}\exp\bigl\{\langle x,b_{j}^{(t)}\rangle-\beta_{j}^{(t)}\bigr\}-\exp\left\{\langle x,b_{j}\rangle-\beta_{j}\right\}\,dx\\
&amp;=-\frac{1}{n}+\lim_{t\rightarrow 0}\frac{1}{t}\sum_{j\in J_{i}}\left[\int_{C_{n,j}}e^{\langle x,b_{j}\rangle-\beta_{j}}\bigl\{e^{t(1-\langle A_{j}^{-1}(x-\alpha_{j}),1_{d}\rangle)}-1\bigr\}\,dx\mathbbm{1}_{\{j_{1}=i\}}\right.\\
&amp;\hskip 142.26378pt+\left.\sum_{l=1}^{d}\int_{C_{n,j}}e^{\langle x,b_{j}\rangle-\beta_{j}}\bigl\{e^{t\langle A_{j}^{-1}(x-\alpha_{j}),e_{l}^{d}\rangle}-1\bigr\}\,dx\mathbbm{1}_{\{j_{l+1}=i\}}\right]\\
&amp;=\partial_{i}(y),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">where to obtain the final line we have made the substitution <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=A_{j}w+\alpha_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=A_{j}w+\alpha_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, after taking the limit as <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(b) If <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, then it can be shown that there exists a
unit coordinate vector <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the
<em id="A2.SS1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">one-sided directional derivative</em> at <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
denoted <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(y;e_{i}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(y;e_{i}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(y;e_{i}^{n})&gt;-\sigma^{\prime}(y;-e_{i}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(y;e_{i}^{n})&gt;-\sigma^{\prime}(y;-e_{i}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is not differentiable at <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. To
show that <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial(y)=(\partial_{1}(y),\ldots,\partial_{n}(y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial(y)=(\partial_{1}(y),\ldots,\partial_{n}(y))</annotation></semantics></math> is a
subgradient of <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, it is enough by Theorem 25.6
of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar (1997)</a></cite> to find, for each <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, a point
<math id="A2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tilde{y}-y\|&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tilde{y}-y\|&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>
and such that <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable at <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla\sigma(\tilde{y})-\partial(y)\|&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla\sigma(\tilde{y})-\partial(y)\|&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be done by
sequentially making small adjustments to the components of <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> in the
same order as that in which the vertices were <em id="A2.SS1.p3.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pushed</em> in
constructing the triangulation.  <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Box" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Box</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A subgradient of <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> at any <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> may be computed
using Proposition <a href="#Thmthm4" title="Proposition 4. ‣ B.1 Non-differentiability of 𝜎 and computation of subgradients ‣ Appendix B Proofs ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, (<a href="#A2.Ex16" title="B.1 Non-differentiability of 𝜎 and computation of subgradients ‣ Appendix B Proofs ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.1 Reformulation ‣ 4 Computation of the maximum likelihood estimator ‣ Maximum likelihood estimation of a multidimensional log-concave density" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) once we have a formula for</p>
<table id="A2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{I}_{d,u}(z)=\int_{T_{d}}w_{u}\exp\biggl(\sum_{r=1}^{d}z_{r}w_{r}\biggr)\,dw," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{I}_{d,u}(z)=\int_{T_{d}}w_{u}\exp\biggl(\sum_{r=1}^{d}z_{r}w_{r}\biggr)\,dw,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
